{"result":0,"text":"Tom set down his pint and rose from the bench. He crossed the taproom through the press of drinkers, some six metres of it, and came up to the bar, where the innkeeper was drawing off another jug for a man at the far end."},
{"result":3,"text":"He leaned in and spoke close to the landlord, above the din but not shouting. \"Two men at the next table have been whispering low and close all this while, and I could not catch a word of it; they looked at me as if they feared I was listening.\" The room was loud, and the innkeeper made out most of it, though a word or two of it was swallowed by the noise; the sense came through whole."},
{"result":5,"text":"The innkeeper looked at Tom a moment, wiping his hands slow on his apron. He had heard tales like this before from men wanting a free pint for their trouble, and something in Tom's manner did not sit right with him. He judged it a lie, or at least a tale dressed up beyond its worth, and he had no proof either way, no more than a stranger's word against two quiet drinkers."},
{"result":4,"text":"Across the room, the younger of the two whisperers had been watching. He saw Tom get up, saw him go to the bar, and saw him lean in and speak with the landlord, and he knew plain enough what it might mean: the carter was telling the man about their whispering."},
{"result":6,"text":"He said nothing to his companion of it, and made his face easy. He stayed where he sat, kept his hands about his pot, and acted as if nothing at all were wrong."},
{"result":7,"text":"The older man, catching nothing of the bar in the noise, followed his companion's lead as he always did, sitting easy and drinking as though the evening were no different from any other."}
],"open":["What the innkeeper says or does next, with Tom standing at the bar and the two men acting innocent across the room."]}

